// What an agent's post asks the owner (design-two-systems §16, 2; design.md part 9): the thread kind's `agent_post`
// handler makes the step, owed by the watcher, which coordd answers itself as it does intake and route. It runs `find`
// below once, statelessly, with the model policy names for the task `asks`, and answers `asks` with what it found.
// The call marks, it never rewrites: each ask and each option is a passage copied from the message. coordd looks each
// up in the message (docket_machine::asks::spans: an option first inside its ask, else anywhere in the message) and
// answers `asks` with where each stands, byte offsets into the post's text, half-open: thread.kind's `mark p marks`
// puts them on the post. What is not found word for word is thrown away; an item whose ask is not found goes, and none
// left is Nothing. A call that fails, or answers out of shape, is Nothing: the post is always shown whole, and a
// missed ask costs the owner only the card. BY is the agent who wrote the post.

type Item {
  ask: Text
  options: [Text]
}

type Mark {
  at: [Int]
  options: [[Int]]
}

call asks(text: Text, by: Agent) -> Nothing | Asks(marks: [Mark])
  task asks

call find(text: Text, by: Agent) -> Nothing | Asks(items: [Item])
  task asks
  template
    An agent wrote the message below to the owner of a project. Find what it asks the owner to answer or decide. When
    the numbered items of the message are what it asks the owner (its questions to him), each numbered item is one
    ask: copy the whole item, from its first word to its last, as one ask, even when it holds several sentences. When
    the numbered items are a plan, steps, findings or anything else it tells him, they are not asks: from them take
    only a sentence that asks the owner something, as one ask. When the message offers ready-made choices to pick from
    (a list of ideas or proposals), the sentence that offers them is an ask too, and the choices are its options, each
    by its short name. When it does not number them, each question is one ask. Under an ask, list the options the
    message offers for it. An option is a short name or phrase of a few words, copied exactly as written, never a
    whole sentence. An option may stand anywhere in the message. An ask with no offered options has an empty list.
    Copy only, character for character. Do not reword, shorten, add, correct or merge. If the message asks the owner
    nothing, answer Nothing.

    The message, from {{ by }}:
    <<<
    {{ text }}
    >>>
